OP-TEE Opaque Keys Examples
==================================

Contents
--------
* About
* Requirements
* Compilation and running
* Application description
* Credits

About
-----
This demo showcases the use of opaque keys in the TrustZone Execution
Environtment (TEE). This new feature aims to enhance the overall security
of the TrustZone by concealing the key material, making it 'opaque' to
all the consumers in the trust chain. Instead of passing around the plain
key material, applications will reference keys through an ID, called a
Key Handle.

Requirements
------------

The following list of components are required for the build and run of this example:
* [optee_os](https://github.com/nxp-auto-linux/optee_os)
* [optee_client](https://github.com/OP-TEE/optee_client)

The compilation of these components is a prerequisite to this example. Manual
build steps instructions are included in the repositories and the Linux BSP
User Manual. For a successfull run of the example, the image on the target
platform must include these OP-TEE components. The easiest way of generating
an OP-TEE-ready image is to use the Yocto build tool. For more details on this
please consult the Linux BSP User Manual.

Compilation and running
-----------------------

From the root folder of this repository enter the following command:  

make CROSS_COMPILE=</path/to/your/toolchain/dir>/bin/aarch64-none-linux-gnu- \
     TA_DEV_KIT_DIR=</path/to/optee_os>/out/arm-plat-s32/export-ta_arm64 \
     TEEC_EXPORT=</path/to/optee_client>/out/export/usr tee_opaque_keys

Please note the two variables TA_DEV_KIT_DIR & TEEC_EXPORT:

1. **TA_DEV_KIT_DIR**  
It must point to the TA Development Kit. As indicated
in the make command, the kit is located in the optee_os repository. Compiling
optee_os will result in the compilation of the development kit as well.

2. **TEEC_EXPORT**  
It must point to the TEE Client API. As indicated
in the make command, the interface is located in the optee_client repository.
Compiling optee_client will result in the compilation of the TEE Client API as well.

The succesfull execution of the make command generates two binaries:
<tee_opaque_keys/host/optee_example_hse_opaque_keys> and
<tee_opaque_keys/ta/5fe52085-5121-4cdf-b800-4964b8dd0c94.ta>.

Running the example: copy the TA binary, i.e 5fe52085-5121-4cdf-b800-4964b8dd0c94.ta
in the rootfs of the target platform at the location </lib/optee_armtz/>. Copy
the normal world application, i.e. optee_example_hse_opaque_keys in the rootfs
of the target platform and run:  
	`./optee_example_hse_embed_handle`

For helpful information on running the example, run the application with the argument
'-h':  
	`./optee_example_hse_embed_handle -h`

Application Description
-----------------------
The TEE Internal Core API is used within the TEE to define a set of functionalities
such as cryptographic operations, secure storage, arithmetic operations etc.
TAs request these services from optee_os through a standardized set of C functions
that are implemented in optee_os.

Some of the cryptographic functions defined in the TEE Internal Core API use a
key to perform their operations. The API mandates that the keys are passed
in plain format from the TAs to optee_os. Therefore, in the case of a TEE exploit,
the key material might leak.

The opaque key feature challenges the status quo and provides and alternative
way of passing keys. Instead of providing the raw key material, the example
references the key through an ID called a Key Handle. To do this, the opaque key
feature relies on the Hardware Secruity Engine's (HSE) capability of storing keys
and using them for crypto operations without exposure to the normal world or
the secure world. The keys are kept in a catalog where each key is uniquely
identified through a Key Handle. Of course, the keys should be imported in the
catalog before using the opaque key feature.

The 4-byte Key Handle is embedded within the same buffer that is normally used
by the API for transmitting the plain key material. A 'magic number' is
prepended to the Key Handle so that optee_os can detect the presence of the
embedded Key Handle within the buffer. After extracting the Key Handle, optee_os
will send the cryptographic request that uses this Key Handle to HSE.

This feature does not hinder in any way the normal use of plain key buffers.
However, the key space is reduced because of the possbile arising collisions:
keys which happen to have the exact bytes as a valid "magic + key_handle"
cannot be used anymore as the TEE will interpret the buffer as an embedded
key handle.

This example is made of two distinct parts:
1. Import the plain key material into the HSE Non-Volatile Memory (NVM) Catalog  
2. Use the Key Handles associated with these keys to perform cryptographic
operations.

The first part uses a Pseudo Trusted Application (PTA) to import some dummy
keys into the HSE NVM Catalog. A PTA is an interface exposed by the optee_os core
and is implemented directly in optee_os, as opposed to TAs which are individual
entities. The example application provides the key material and the key
information that is made of key flags (used for specifying key attributes, usage or
permissions), key type and the key handle to the PTA according to its
interface defined in <optee_os/lib/libutee/include/pta_hse_kp.h>. The success of
this operation means that the keys have been imported in the key catalog and can
now be referenced through their specific Key Handles.

The second part performs three cryptographic operations: AES-ECB cipher, RSA NOPAD
encrypt & decrypt and ECC P-192 sign & verify. Through these operations, the
example showcases how the key handle is embedded within the TEE buffers.

